// Generic instantiation and macro expansion that never terminate.
//
// Split from the other three type-check scenarios because the mechanism is
// neither arity nor inference: each of these is well formed and would be
// expanded forever. A generic that instantiates itself at a larger type
// argument never returns to a node it has already analyzed -- every instance is
// a fresh clone with new arguments -- so the marks that catch a declaration
// analyzing itself cannot see it. Nesting depth is the only thing that can, and
// reaching the limit is what these three provoke.
//
// Every one of them was a stack overflow with no diagnostic before the limit
// existed, which is a failure no scenario can assert: the compiler dies before
// it reports anything. That is why this file is worth its own scenario rather
// than a line in an existing one.
//
// The three are here together because they are one mechanism reached three
// ways: the generic instantiation path, and the two macro expansion paths, a
// name standing for its body and a call substituting arguments into it. A guard
// removed from any one of them brings the crash back for that path alone.

// -------- generic instantiation --------

struct Box[T] {
  v T
}

// Each call instantiates 'recur' one 'Box' deeper than the call that reached
// it, so the type argument grows without limit. Both instantiation sites are
// refused: the depth is reached inside the innermost one, and the call wrapping
// it is at the limit too as the refusal unwinds.
//
// The box is built on its own line and the recursive call takes its type by
// inference, so that each line carries one instantiation. Written as a single
// expression the line would carry three, which no annotation can tell apart.
fn recur[T](x T) T {
  imm boxed = Box[T][x]        //~ ErrorInstDepth:18 "nested more than"
  recur(boxed).v               //~ ErrorInstDepth:8 "nested more than"
}

fn useGeneric() i64 {
  recur[i64](3i64)
}

// -------- macro expansion, parameterless --------

// A macro's body is cloned into the use site and analyzed there, so a body
// naming the macro expands again, and again.
macro selfNamed {
  selfNamed                    //~ ErrorInstDepth:3 "nested more than"
}

fn useMacroName() {
  imm a = selfNamed
}

// -------- macro expansion, with arguments --------

// The same, through the path that substitutes arguments for parameters.
macro selfCalled[p] {
  selfCalled[p]                //~ ErrorInstDepth:13 "nested more than"
}

fn useMacroCall() {
  imm b = selfCalled[1i64]
}
